    {
      "id": "s2",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Three components, one system",
      "narration": "The endocannabinoid system has three parts. The receptors, CB1 and CB2, sit in cell membranes and detect the signal. The ligands, anandamide and 2-AG, are the signal itself. And a set of enzymes controls the signal in time: some enzymes build the ligands when they are needed, and others destroy them a moment later. Because the ligands are made on demand and removed quickly, the whole system is designed for brief, local messages rather than long-lasting broadcasts. Understanding the enzymes is therefore essential to understanding the system.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "Receptors: CB1 and CB2 detect the signal",
          "at": 0.08
        },
        {
          "text": "Ligands: anandamide and 2-AG are the signal",
          "at": 0.25
        },
        {
          "text": "Enzymes: synthesis on demand, rapid degradation",
          "at": 0.45
        },
        {
          "text": "Designed for brief, local messages",
          "at": 0.7
        }
      ],
