    {
      "id": "s3",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Making the ligands",
      "narration": "Both endocannabinoids are cut out of membrane lipids. Anandamide is produced from a phospholipid precursor called N-acyl-phosphatidylethanolamine (NAPE), mainly by the enzyme NAPE-PLD, although several alternative routes exist. Two A G is produced in two steps: first, the enzyme phospholipase C-beta (PLCβ) generates diacylglycerol (DAG), and then diacylglycerol lipase (DAGL), releases 2-AG. These enzymes are switched on by a rise in calcium inside the cell, or by activation of certain other receptors. That is what makes the system responsive: the ligand appears only when the cell is active.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "Anandamide: NAPE → NAPE-PLD → anandamide (plus alternative routes)",
          "at": 0.1
        },
        {
          "text": "2-AG: PLCβ makes DAG → DAGL releases 2-AG",
          "at": 0.4
        },
        {
          "text": "Triggered by a rise in intracellular calcium or receptor activation",
          "at": 0.65
        },
        {
          "text": "Ligand appears only when the cell is active",
          "at": 0.85
        }
      ],
