    {
      "id": "s4",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Removing the ligands",
      "narration": "Signals must also end. Anandamide is destroyed mainly by fatty acid amide hydrolase (FAAH), an enzyme identified by Benjamin Cravatt's group in 1996; it splits anandamide into arachidonic acid and ethanolamine. Two A G is destroyed mainly by monoacylglycerol lipase (MAGL), characterised in 2002, which splits it into arachidonic acid and glycerol. Other enzymes, including ABHD6, ABHD12 and cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2), contribute as well. Because degradation is fast, an endocannabinoid signal normally lasts only seconds, and stays close to the place where it was made.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "Anandamide → FAAH (Cravatt, 1996) → arachidonic acid + ethanolamine",
          "at": 0.1
        },
        {
          "text": "2-AG → MAGL (2002) → arachidonic acid + glycerol",
          "at": 0.4
        },
        {
          "text": "Also ABHD6, ABHD12, COX-2",
          "at": 0.65
        },
        {
          "text": "Fast degradation: signals last seconds and stay local",
          "at": 0.82
        }
      ],
