    {
      "id": "s5",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Signalling backwards: the discovery",
      "narration": "Where in the synapse does all this happen? The answer came in 2001 from three laboratories working independently: Rachel Wilson and Roger Nicoll in San Francisco, Takako Ohno-Shosaku and Masanobu Kano in Japan, and Anatol Kreitzer and Wade Regehr in Boston. Each found that when a postsynaptic neuron is strongly activated, it briefly suppresses the inputs it receives. The messenger responsible was an endocannabinoid, and the direction was the reverse of the usual one: the signal travelled from the receiving neuron back to the sending neuron. This is called retrograde signalling.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "2001: three independent groups (Wilson & Nicoll; Ohno-Shosaku, Maejima & Kano; Kreitzer & Regehr)",
          "at": 0.1
        },
        {
          "text": "An activated postsynaptic neuron briefly suppresses its own inputs",
          "at": 0.45
        },
        {
          "text": "Messenger: an endocannabinoid",
          "at": 0.65
        },
        {
          "text": "Direction: from receiving neuron back to sending neuron → retrograde",
          "at": 0.8
        }
      ],
