    {
      "id": "s6",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Retrograde signalling, step by step",
      "narration": "Here is the sequence. First, the postsynaptic neuron is activated and calcium enters it. Second, the calcium switches on the synthesising enzymes, and 2-AG is produced from the membrane. Third, 2-AG moves backwards across the synaptic gap. Fourth, it binds CB1 receptors on the presynaptic terminal. Fifth, CB1, through its G protein, closes calcium channels and opens potassium channels in the terminal. Sixth, with less calcium entering, the terminal releases less neurotransmitter. If the terminal releases GABA, the effect is called DSI; if it releases glutamate, DSE. In both cases, the receiving cell has tuned down its own input.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "1. Postsynaptic activation → Ca²⁺ enters",
          "at": 0.05
        },
        {
          "text": "2–3. Enzymes make 2-AG → it travels backwards across the synapse",
          "at": 0.25
        },
        {
          "text": "4–5. 2-AG binds presynaptic CB1 → Ca²⁺ channels close, K⁺ channels open",
          "at": 0.5
        },
        {
          "text": "6. Less transmitter released (GABA → DSI; glutamate → DSE)",
          "at": 0.78
        }
      ],
