    {
      "id": "s7",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "A feedback system for synapses",
      "narration": "Retrograde signalling makes the endocannabinoid system a feedback mechanism: it lets a neuron adjust the strength of the connections it receives, according to its own activity. The adjustment can be brief, lasting seconds, or it can trigger longer-lasting changes in synaptic strength, a form of plasticity called long-term depression. Because CB1 receptors sit on both inhibitory and excitatory terminals throughout the cortex, hippocampus, basal ganglia and cerebellum, this feedback operates across many brain circuits. Outside the brain, CB2 on immune cells responds to endocannabinoids as part of immune signalling.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "A neuron tunes its own inputs according to its activity",
          "at": 0.08
        },
        {
          "text": "Short-term (seconds) or long-term changes (long-term depression)",
          "at": 0.3
        },
        {
          "text": "CB1 on inhibitory and excitatory terminals across many circuits",
          "at": 0.55
        },
        {
          "text": "CB2 on immune cells: part of immune signalling",
          "at": 0.82
        }
      ],
