    {
      "id": "s8",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Where plant cannabinoids fit in",
      "narration": "Mechanistically, the plant molecules enter this picture in different ways. THC is a partial agonist at CB1 and CB2: it occupies the same receptors as 2-AG and anandamide and activates them, though not to the same degree as 2-AG. The main difference is timing and location. An endocannabinoid is made at one synapse, acts there, and is gone within seconds. THC arrives through the bloodstream, is not produced or removed by the synapse's own enzymes, and therefore reaches CB1 receptors across many circuits at the same time. CBD, in contrast, binds CB1 only weakly, and its reported actions involve other targets, among them the TRPV1 ion channel and the enzyme FAAH.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "THC: partial agonist at CB1 and CB2; occupies the same receptors as 2-AG",
          "at": 0.05
        },
        {
          "text": "Endocannabinoid: made at one synapse, acts there, gone in seconds",
          "at": 0.35
        },
        {
          "text": "THC: arrives via bloodstream, not cleared by synaptic enzymes, reaches many circuits at once",
          "at": 0.55
        },
        {
          "text": "CBD: weak at CB1; reported targets include TRPV1 and FAAH",
          "at": 0.82
        }
